<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narayanhiti Palace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narayanhiti Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> was the main residence of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. The present building, designed by the American architect Benjamin Polk, was completed around 1970 on the site of an older palace. On 1 June 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> and much of the royal family were killed there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipendra-of-nepal">Crown Prince Dipendra</a>. After the abolition of the monarchy in 2008 it became a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace.jpg" alt="Narayanhiti Palace" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suraj Belbase · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narayanhiti_Palace_Museum,_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The palace stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, east of the Kaiser Mahal and next to Thamel. Its name joins Narayana, an epithet of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, whose temple faces the palace, and hiti, the Newar word for a water spout, after a stone drinking fountain east of the main entrance that features in local legends. The building has a floor space of 3,794 square metres in three parts, the guest, state and private wings, with interiors in a late Victorian style and rooms named after the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was first home to Chautariya families, among them Prime Minister Fateh Jung Shah, who was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-massacre">Kot massacre</a> of 19 September 1846. Colonel Ranodip Singh Kunwar, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jung Bahadur Rana</a>, then took it over and, after becoming prime minister in 1877, enlarged it into a lavish palace; he was assassinated in its southern wing during the coup of 22 November 1885. His successor Bir Shumsher had the old palace pulled down in 1886 and a new one built by the architect Jogbir Sthapit for his son-in-law, King Prithvi Bir Bikram Shah, which moved the royal residence here from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a>.</p><p>The 1934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> earthquake damaged that palace and killed two infant daughters of King Tribhuvan. King Mahendra had it replaced from 1963 with a building in Nepalese styles meant as a national symbol; its housewarming was held on 27 February 1970 for the wedding of Crown Prince Birendra. Among those killed in the royal massacre of 1 June 2001 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aishwarya-of-nepal">Queen Aishwarya</a>. After the 2006 revolution the last king, Gyanendra, left the palace on 11 June 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former palace is now the Narayanhiti Palace Museum, where the royal crown jewels have been shown since October 2018. Visitors see Kaski Sadan, the reception hall where kings gave audience and prime ministers took their oath, and the throne room, Gorkha Baithak, whose pagoda-style ceiling rises 60 feet above the place of the throne. A Garuda outside the palace's Narayan temple is thought to date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narayanhiti Palace Museum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 June 2001 Crown Prince Dipendra shot dead King Birendra, Queen Aishwarya and other members of the royal family at the <b>Narayanhiti Palace</b> before dying of his own wounds, and Birendra’s brother Gyanendra became king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
